00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:39.720
MARIA LAURA SCURI:
No, not really.

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:42.620
So [INAUDIBLE] have
always been a core for us,

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:45.530
but also for game
publishers, as well,

00:08:45.530 --> 00:08:47.855
because we work closely with
a lot of game publishers.

00:08:47.855 --> 00:08:49.230
So one of the
things that we have

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:51.920
is a very smooth
integration with the game,

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:53.217
so it's very easy.

00:08:53.217 --> 00:08:54.800
Basically, with the
click of a button,

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:56.540
you get to FACEIT to the game.

00:08:56.540 --> 00:09:00.080
And things are not always
that smooth when you actually

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:03.020
put several people that don't
know each other that well

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:05.840
into the same online scenario.

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:10.080
I think toxicity has always
been a big issue in gaming.

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:12.510
It's definitely an
issue for us, as well.

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:14.600
I think it's
probably due to some

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:16.940
of the aspects
about matchmaking,

00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:19.840
or in general, the
gaming experience.

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:22.130
On FACEIT, for example,
just to give you an example,

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:26.820
we have 800,000 concurrent
players at the same time.

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:30.080
You need just 10 of them to
make a match, and honestly,

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:32.450
the likelihood that
after this match

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:35.780
you're going to encounter again
exactly the same other nine

00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:38.240
players, it's close to zero.

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:40.270
There are probably
social inhibitions

00:09:40.270 --> 00:09:43.130
that you would have in
another environment,

00:09:43.130 --> 00:09:44.900
like probably at
school, they are not

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:48.470
going to just go into the class
and start insulting somebody

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:51.350
because then you're faced
with that every day, no?

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:54.080
But in gaming,
while, I mean, I'm

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:56.720
probably spending like
30 minutes of my life

00:09:56.720 --> 00:09:58.310
with these other
nine people, and I

00:09:58.310 --> 00:10:00.680
think the other guy
has just done something

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:01.925
that is really stupid--

00:10:01.925 --> 00:10:02.950
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:02.950 --> 00:10:05.810
--then I'm probably
going to say that to him,

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:07.580
and I'm probably
actually going to say

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:09.860
that in not such a polite way.

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:11.750
And I think that's,
in the end, it's

00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:14.490
like an issue to
online behavior,

00:10:14.490 --> 00:10:16.310
especially anywhere
you're online.

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:18.580
You're also like--
with video games,

00:10:18.580 --> 00:10:21.530
in general, but in other
situations as well.

00:10:21.530 --> 00:10:24.170
You are yourself,
but you're also

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:27.110
using an avatar to go
around, and I think already

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:29.300
that puts some of the
social inhibitions

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:31.160
that you maybe have
in other context,

00:10:31.160 --> 00:10:32.638
those are lowered down.

00:10:32.638 --> 00:10:34.680
JON FAUST: So you've got
a whole group of people,

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:37.100
everyone with, kind of,
lowered social inhibitions

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:38.870
in a stressful environment.

00:10:38.870 --> 00:10:42.437
Things can go poorly
pretty easily, huh?

00:10:42.437 --> 00:10:44.270
MARIA LAURA SCURI: Yes,
if you get, already,

00:10:44.270 --> 00:10:46.580
people starting with
lower inhibitions.

00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:48.530
And then, you add, on top that--

00:10:48.530 --> 00:10:50.540
especially on FACEIT,
the environment

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:54.260
is really competitive
because of different reasons.

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:57.810
Like on one side,
we have a pathway

00:10:57.810 --> 00:11:00.620
to become a professional
player and to actually get

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:02.610
the occasion to be noticed.

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:05.790
So as you rank up on FACEIT,
you can end up in our FACEIT Pro

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:07.950
League, where, for
"Counter-Strike," for example,

00:11:07.950 --> 00:11:10.700
lots of professional players
have actually been talent

00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:12.100
scouted from there.

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:15.740
In some tournaments you might
be competing for money, as well.

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:17.430
So I think the two
things together,

00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:18.910
then you could
have a bomb that's

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:21.160
about to explode, in that way.

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:23.120
There's lots of tension.

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:25.510
Especially if you're seeing
that the match is not really

00:11:25.510 --> 00:11:29.620
going in your direction, there's
lots of tension that builds up.

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:32.980
And the way that's
expressed in some cases--

00:11:32.980 --> 00:11:35.890
not all the times, but
quite a few cases--

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:38.140
can be like pretty
toxic, honestly.

00:11:38.140 --> 00:11:41.110
JON FOUST: So I'm a very
competitive gamer, myself.

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:43.450
I actually had a meeting
earlier this week with Brian.

00:11:43.450 --> 00:11:46.150
And I actually told him that
I was gaming for 20 hours

00:11:46.150 --> 00:11:46.720
straight.

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:49.570
And I can tell you that
after gaming for 20 hours,

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:51.520
your personality will
definitely change,

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:53.230
although it's not
really you that people

00:11:53.230 --> 00:11:54.430
see when they're gaming.

00:11:54.430 --> 00:11:57.070
I can say that,
after a while, once I

00:11:57.070 --> 00:11:58.570
was on a pretty
good winning streak,

00:11:58.570 --> 00:12:01.600
and I was just really excited
to finally be back to my rank

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:03.400
that I believe I belong in--

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:06.110
I can tell you that you start
to judge people quite often.

00:12:06.110 --> 00:12:08.330
So the question
I really have is,

00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:10.530
what are you really
doing to address

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:11.530
the toxicity in matches?

00:12:11.530 --> 00:12:13.720
Because there are different
forms of toxicity--

00:12:13.720 --> 00:12:16.870
throwing games, there's
verbal, there's text.

00:12:16.870 --> 00:12:19.090
So what are you really
doing to address that?

00:12:19.090 --> 00:12:20.640
And how does FACEIT address it?

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:22.140
MARIA LAURA SCURI:
So I think we are

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:24.460
trying to get quite an
innovative approach, when

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:25.380
it comes to that.

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:28.150
So firstly, we started
with text chat.

00:12:28.150 --> 00:12:29.950
And that's something
that we actually

00:12:29.950 --> 00:12:32.310
had a case study
published by Jigsaw

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:35.680
of how we are using and
integrating Perspective API--

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:39.070
one of the Google
tools-- into our system.

00:12:39.070 --> 00:12:42.620
But on the other hand, we do
know that there's more to it.

00:12:42.620 --> 00:12:45.700
So text is probably
just one of the ways

00:12:45.700 --> 00:12:48.220
that toxicity is
clearly surfacing.

00:12:48.220 --> 00:12:49.760
But, as you were
saying, there are

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:53.960
just so many different behaviors
that you can annoy people with.

00:12:53.960 --> 00:12:57.140
So if we take "Counter-Strike,"
you could be griefing.

00:12:57.140 --> 00:13:00.330
So you maybe are just blocking
the passage to somebody.

00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:02.965
There's voice chat, you
could kill a teammate.

00:13:02.965 --> 00:13:04.340
So there's many
different things.

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:05.950
So what we are
really trying to do

00:13:05.950 --> 00:13:08.590
is, basically, build a
comprehensive system that

00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:12.100
is going to be able to
analyze and understand each

00:13:12.100 --> 00:13:13.900
of these different behaviors.

00:13:13.900 --> 00:13:17.830
So we see text chat is just
like the very first milestone

00:13:17.830 --> 00:13:19.720
for what we are
actually building.

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:22.850
And one of the next steps
that we have, for example,

00:13:22.850 --> 00:13:27.760
is we are building a UI that's
going to involve our community

00:13:27.760 --> 00:13:31.060
to actually help us understand
what they see as toxic.

00:13:31.060 --> 00:13:33.550
Because I think that's also
the other problem that you're

00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:36.370
actually trying to
solve is also toxicity

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:39.780
is a perception of toxicity.

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:44.380
So you don't have a clear cut
"that was definitely toxic,"

00:13:44.380 --> 00:13:47.680
especially when it comes
to certain behavior.

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:49.210
There's two things
that you always

00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:52.360
need to evaluate-- that's
intention, on one side,

00:13:52.360 --> 00:13:54.790
and actually if that
was perceived as toxic.

00:13:54.790 --> 00:13:57.080
Because there's some
situations where,

00:13:57.080 --> 00:14:00.520
for example, even if what
you're saying could be,

00:14:00.520 --> 00:14:02.440
in some scenarios,
considered toxic,

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:04.360
but, maybe, if I'm in
a group of friends,

00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:06.820
then that's qualified as banter.

00:14:06.820 --> 00:14:09.400
So there's all these
different nuances

00:14:09.400 --> 00:14:12.420
that are not that easy to
understand on one side.

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:14.980
And then on the other side,
what we're really trying to do

00:14:14.980 --> 00:14:18.010
and what our system,
basically, looks like-- we

00:14:18.010 --> 00:14:20.780
are getting data from
different sources.

00:14:20.780 --> 00:14:25.750
So we take text chat, we take
voice chat, we also, basically,

00:14:25.750 --> 00:14:30.040
have the stream, an HTTP
stream of every event that

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:31.420
happened in the game.

00:14:31.420 --> 00:14:33.830
And we are recording
this set of events.

00:14:33.830 --> 00:14:35.830
So we know, for
example, if, in a match,

00:14:35.830 --> 00:14:39.280
somebody started a vote
to kick somebody out,

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:40.960
we know if there
was a [INAUDIBLE]..

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:43.210
We know if there
were other signals.

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:45.440
On the basis of all
this different sources,

00:14:45.440 --> 00:14:46.850
the first step
that we want to do

00:14:46.850 --> 00:14:50.170
is, basically, create
cases that our players

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:51.650
are going to help us label.

00:14:51.650 --> 00:14:55.300
So the first step is,
let's make this interface.

00:14:55.300 --> 00:14:59.890
This system that we actually are
going to release in, probably,

00:14:59.890 --> 00:15:01.670
a month it's going to go out.

00:15:01.670 --> 00:15:03.400
But the first step
would be, let's

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:05.860
get the input from
our players to help

00:15:05.860 --> 00:15:07.720
us actually understand
what's going on

00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:09.700
and label all this data.

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:12.660
And then this is going
to fit, probably,

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:15.340
into different models
that are going to then

00:15:15.340 --> 00:15:17.350
be able to analyze each stream.

00:15:17.350 --> 00:15:20.590
And then, finally, we can get
a comprehensive understanding

00:15:20.590 --> 00:15:22.548
of what really went on.

00:15:22.548 --> 00:15:24.340
BRIAN DORSEY: So just
measuring all of this

00:15:24.340 --> 00:15:25.690
is a real challenge.

00:15:25.690 --> 00:15:27.430
You described a
couple of scenarios.

00:15:27.430 --> 00:15:29.140
So those are some of
your inputs and you

00:15:29.140 --> 00:15:30.220
get some of the labeling.

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:31.450
Is it all just labeling?

00:15:31.450 --> 00:15:33.575
MARIA LAURA SCURI: Labeling
is just the first step.

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:39.370
So if we take the chat
toxicity as an example case,

00:15:39.370 --> 00:15:43.510
we send almost 200 million
messages to Perspective API.

00:15:43.510 --> 00:15:46.330
For each of those messages,
we got the score back.

00:15:46.330 --> 00:15:47.050
Then we went in.

00:15:47.050 --> 00:15:49.480
We actually analyzed
the discourse

00:15:49.480 --> 00:15:53.530
we were getting, because the
score, by itself, might not

00:15:53.530 --> 00:15:55.570
necessarily mean that--

00:15:55.570 --> 00:15:59.470
of course, the higher the score
is close to one, the more toxic

00:15:59.470 --> 00:16:01.750
that message should
be perceived.

00:16:01.750 --> 00:16:04.180
But the other part that we
were interested in as well,

00:16:04.180 --> 00:16:07.730
from our side, was to also
try to understand, OK,

00:16:07.730 --> 00:16:10.330
what is the full
sentiment in that match?

00:16:10.330 --> 00:16:12.550
So it's not just about
the single comment,

00:16:12.550 --> 00:16:17.200
but it's also that toxicity
usually builds other toxicity.

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:20.860
So we also wanted to take the
whole chat into consideration.

00:16:20.860 --> 00:16:24.370
So on top of that, we
actually built our own machine

00:16:24.370 --> 00:16:28.450
learning model to, basically,
get to a final score

00:16:28.450 --> 00:16:31.000
for that user in that match.

00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:34.510
On the basis of that score,
then Minerva, which is,

00:16:34.510 --> 00:16:36.670
basically, our admin AI--

00:16:36.670 --> 00:16:38.740
so we are, basically,
just trying

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:41.080
to replicate what a
human moderator would do.

00:16:41.080 --> 00:16:45.610
If we had enough
people to monitor

00:16:45.610 --> 00:16:49.600
5,000 concurrent matches or
10,000 concurrent matches,

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:50.620
then you probably would.

00:16:50.620 --> 00:16:53.590
But that doesn't
really scale that well.

00:16:53.590 --> 00:16:56.690
So on the basis of
these final scores, then

00:16:56.690 --> 00:16:58.210
Minerva takes a certain action.

00:16:58.210 --> 00:17:01.250
It either warns the
player or it issues a ban.

00:17:01.250 --> 00:17:03.050
I think it was two
sides of things--

00:17:03.050 --> 00:17:05.380
one thing was,
firstly, implementing

00:17:05.380 --> 00:17:07.690
a perspective in
this case, then it

00:17:07.690 --> 00:17:12.000
was a lot of understanding
what that score actually means.

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:14.349
But also what that
score means relative

00:17:14.349 --> 00:17:16.310
to our specific population.

00:17:16.310 --> 00:17:19.329
Because especially with
a system like Perspective

00:17:19.329 --> 00:17:23.319
that was built for
a broader scenario--

00:17:23.319 --> 00:17:26.470
like an online
perception of chat

00:17:26.470 --> 00:17:29.920
and how each comment can
affect the conversation--

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:35.170
I think it's also important to
relate that with your own use

00:17:35.170 --> 00:17:35.770
case.

00:17:35.770 --> 00:17:39.040
Because, probably,
moderating a gaming community

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:45.020
of 18 to 25 year olds is very
different than my grandma

00:17:45.020 --> 00:17:49.720
that, today, wanted to read
an article on the "Times"

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:54.130
and was shocked about a
specific comment that,

00:17:54.130 --> 00:17:56.350
honestly, in the
context of gaming,

00:17:56.350 --> 00:17:58.660
would have been
totally accepted.

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:02.170
So I think there's always a
little bit of differentiation

00:18:02.170 --> 00:18:05.260
that you need to do it and
that you need to consider.

00:18:05.260 --> 00:18:07.900
And then I think,
on top of that,

00:18:07.900 --> 00:18:10.390
now you've got your
machine learning model,

00:18:10.390 --> 00:18:11.720
you've got your metrics.

00:18:11.720 --> 00:18:13.660
So in our case it's
this metric that

00:18:13.660 --> 00:18:17.200
tries to define, let's
say, the level of chat

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:19.450
toxicity of the user in there.

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:21.400
And then you could
have another metric

00:18:21.400 --> 00:18:25.090
that is defining the
level of voice toxicity

00:18:25.090 --> 00:18:26.317
the user had in the match.

00:18:26.317 --> 00:18:28.650
And then you are going to
have a third metric that tries

00:18:28.650 --> 00:18:31.773
to understand their behavior.

00:18:31.773 --> 00:18:33.190
You will need to
try to understand

00:18:33.190 --> 00:18:37.120
how these metrics are going
to coexist with each other.

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:39.610
In our case, what
we are trying to do

00:18:39.610 --> 00:18:43.820
is punish the behavior
rather than the user.

00:18:43.820 --> 00:18:46.810
So we are trying to make
a distinction about,

00:18:46.810 --> 00:18:50.540
you are being toxic versus
you did something toxic.

00:18:50.540 --> 00:18:52.990
And we are trying to go
in the second direction.

00:18:52.990 --> 00:18:55.270
So we are really trying
to say you did something

00:18:55.270 --> 00:18:57.040
toxic that you
shouldn't have done,

00:18:57.040 --> 00:18:59.690
but that doesn't
qualify you as a person.

00:18:59.690 --> 00:19:01.450
So in our case, in
this example, like

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:03.820
if you were toxic in
the chat and then,

00:19:03.820 --> 00:19:06.370
probably, like then
killed somebody,

00:19:06.370 --> 00:19:08.770
you would be issued
two different bans.

00:19:08.770 --> 00:19:10.420
And those bans
and those warnings

00:19:10.420 --> 00:19:12.340
would be targeted
to the behavior.

00:19:12.340 --> 00:19:14.200
Because ultimately,
what we are trying to do

00:19:14.200 --> 00:19:17.410
is also pinpoint exactly
what you did wrong,

00:19:17.410 --> 00:19:20.050
and allow you to
understand what happened,

00:19:20.050 --> 00:19:22.070
and, possibly, learn from it.

00:19:22.070 --> 00:19:24.430
That's the part that we
are really interested in.

00:19:24.430 --> 00:19:26.740
And what we really want
to try to do is, actually,

00:19:26.740 --> 00:19:29.730
finding a way of instead
of punishing toxicity,

00:19:29.730 --> 00:19:31.420
can we prevent toxicity?

00:19:31.420 --> 00:19:34.090
And can we do something
that actually changes

00:19:34.090 --> 00:19:36.340
the behavior of our
user, of our players,

00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:38.770
and, potentially, changes
it for the better?

00:19:38.770 --> 00:19:40.780
So for us, it was
really important.

00:19:40.780 --> 00:19:43.740
And that's something that I
was great with Perspective.

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:45.670
For example, for the
text chat and the model

00:19:45.670 --> 00:19:47.530
we are developing,
it was actually also

00:19:47.530 --> 00:19:51.100
being able to tell the
users, this is what you said,

00:19:51.100 --> 00:19:52.690
this is how it
escalated, and this

00:19:52.690 --> 00:19:54.270
is why it's not acceptable.

00:19:54.270 --> 00:19:57.160
And we really valued that
feedback that we can give.

00:19:57.160 --> 00:19:58.270
JON FOUST: I'm kind of curious
because you've mentioned

00:19:58.270 --> 00:20:00.460
that you're currently
using our Perspective API,

00:20:00.460 --> 00:20:02.740
and you're using this to
build out a bunch of models.

00:20:02.740 --> 00:20:04.660
I'm curious, aside
from Perspective API,

00:20:04.660 --> 00:20:06.910
are you utilizing any
other GCP products

00:20:06.910 --> 00:20:08.260
to address issues in gaming?

00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:09.260
MARIA LAURA SCURI: Yeah.

00:20:09.260 --> 00:20:10.945
A lot of them.

00:20:10.945 --> 00:20:12.820
JON FOUST: I've always
thought about toxicity

00:20:12.820 --> 00:20:14.940
as something that is a
real big issue in gaming.

00:20:14.940 --> 00:20:16.150
Being interested
in it myself, how

00:20:16.150 --> 00:20:17.817
do you deal with it
culturally, as well?

00:20:17.817 --> 00:20:20.530
Because you can see that,
maybe, in certain cultures,

00:20:20.530 --> 00:20:22.570
certain phrasing
and certain words

00:20:22.570 --> 00:20:25.510
are not considered not
an insult or toxic.

00:20:25.510 --> 00:20:30.300
Whereas, in another country, it
may be completely unacceptable.

00:20:30.300 --> 00:20:32.850
MARIA LAURA SCURI: So in
terms of GCP products,

00:20:32.850 --> 00:20:35.860
we are using a lot
of Google BigQuery.

00:20:35.860 --> 00:20:39.430
It's, basically, the basis
of any machine learning

00:20:39.430 --> 00:20:41.320
that we want to
do, because that's

00:20:41.320 --> 00:20:44.090
where we do all our
data exploration.

00:20:44.090 --> 00:20:46.700
So everything that
happens on the platform,

00:20:46.700 --> 00:20:47.890
it's fed into BigQuery.

00:20:47.890 --> 00:20:52.000
So on BigQuery, we, basically,
have all of our data warehouse.

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:53.380
It lives there.

00:20:53.380 --> 00:20:55.930
Another tool that we are
using a lot is Looker.

00:20:55.930 --> 00:20:58.910
Google Cloud, I think, acquired
it a couple of months ago.

00:20:58.910 --> 00:21:01.840
So for example, we are using
data that a lot for data

00:21:01.840 --> 00:21:02.900
visualization.

00:21:02.900 --> 00:21:06.940
So we usually do the first
pre-processing inside BigQuery,

00:21:06.940 --> 00:21:09.760
and then just plot
things in Looker.

00:21:09.760 --> 00:21:12.710
Or we're a lot Cloud
Datalab as well,

00:21:12.710 --> 00:21:15.090
especially because that
really simplifies a lot.

00:21:15.090 --> 00:21:17.500
When you are tackling
an issue like toxicity,

00:21:17.500 --> 00:21:21.000
we are tracking it
in a team of people.

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:22.510
So it was really
important for us

00:21:22.510 --> 00:21:27.010
to be able to collaborate
on Jupyter Notebook as well.

00:21:27.010 --> 00:21:29.380
If you don't use Cloud
Datalab, but you're

00:21:29.380 --> 00:21:31.880
building your own
Notebook locally,

00:21:31.880 --> 00:21:34.600
then you need to send
that file into an HTML,

00:21:34.600 --> 00:21:36.512
or you need to download
that file somehow,

00:21:36.512 --> 00:21:38.220
and then send it to
the other person that

00:21:38.220 --> 00:21:41.315
needs to work on it, then
you need to share it.

00:21:41.315 --> 00:21:43.520
You might be sharing
the code base in GitHub,

00:21:43.520 --> 00:21:45.100
but sometimes that's not enough.

00:21:45.100 --> 00:21:48.310
So for example, Datalab
was really useful for us

00:21:48.310 --> 00:21:51.130
because it's integrated
directly with BigQuery.

00:21:51.130 --> 00:21:53.103
So you can easily
pull SQL queries

00:21:53.103 --> 00:21:55.270
that you would run on
BigQuery, and you can put them

00:21:55.270 --> 00:21:56.330
inside your Notebook.

00:21:56.330 --> 00:21:57.885
So that's super easy.

00:21:57.885 --> 00:22:00.010
And then on the other side,
it was very good for us

00:22:00.010 --> 00:22:01.420
because you can share them.

00:22:01.420 --> 00:22:04.190
And, basically, you can have
multiple people working on it.

00:22:04.190 --> 00:22:05.410
So that was great.

00:22:05.410 --> 00:22:08.870
Then, of course, we are
using the Cloud AI platform.

00:22:08.870 --> 00:22:12.260
So we are building our
machine learning model

00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:13.360
and it's living there.

00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:15.340
We are using mainly TensorFlow.

00:22:15.340 --> 00:22:17.470
For example, one
of the things that

00:22:17.470 --> 00:22:19.840
really helped there
is, on FACEIT, we

00:22:19.840 --> 00:22:21.710
generate lots of data.

00:22:21.710 --> 00:22:24.450
For example, Perspective--
we had 200 million messages

00:22:24.450 --> 00:22:28.450
that we wanted to analyze
and process them quickly.

00:22:28.450 --> 00:22:30.280
You can simply do that locally.

00:22:30.280 --> 00:22:33.960
So being on Cloud AI and being
able to spawn a machine, where

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:35.950
you can, basically,
process as many data

00:22:35.950 --> 00:22:37.600
as you want, depending
on the machine

00:22:37.600 --> 00:22:38.770
you're actually spawning.

00:22:38.770 --> 00:22:40.540
It's simplified
the process a lot.

00:22:40.540 --> 00:22:42.420
I think it took a
lot of headaches

00:22:42.420 --> 00:22:46.210
away that you don't really want
the data science to think of.

00:22:46.210 --> 00:22:48.520
When it comes to
architecture, you

00:22:48.520 --> 00:22:52.000
don't want to have DevOps
experts or data science

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:55.740
teams thinking about how to
set up a virtual machine.

00:22:55.740 --> 00:22:58.060
You just want to get
that done, to get that

00:22:58.060 --> 00:23:01.030
with a click, which is
something that you can do

00:23:01.030 --> 00:23:03.430
on Cloud AI, which was great.

00:23:03.430 --> 00:23:05.170
And then, actually,
before, we were

00:23:05.170 --> 00:23:08.320
using a tool of a competitor,
but we've seen today,

00:23:08.320 --> 00:23:10.780
on your Cloud AI,
that you actually

00:23:10.780 --> 00:23:12.470
have a tool for data labeling.

00:23:12.470 --> 00:23:15.360
So we have started testing that
out, which is in beta right.

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:18.310
So we have a data set being
labeled right now on there.

00:23:18.310 --> 00:23:19.810
JON FOUST: I'm still
kind of curious

00:23:19.810 --> 00:23:21.670
about those cultural
differences and how can

00:23:21.670 --> 00:23:23.968
you consider what's
toxic and what's not.

00:23:23.968 --> 00:23:26.260
MARIA LAURA SCURI: So I think
there are several things.

00:23:26.260 --> 00:23:29.260
So on one side, when it
comes to Perspective API,

00:23:29.260 --> 00:23:31.850
it actually has models
in different languages.

00:23:31.850 --> 00:23:36.190
So right now, it supports
Spanish, French, and English.

00:23:36.190 --> 00:23:37.870
And then there's
three models that

00:23:37.870 --> 00:23:41.140
are in beta, which is, I
think, Italian, Portuguese,

00:23:41.140 --> 00:23:42.150
and another one.

00:23:42.150 --> 00:23:43.870
Then we have been
talking with them a lot

00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:47.080
about Russian, because we have
a lot of Russian new users

00:23:47.080 --> 00:23:50.200
and that's one thing that
we need to factor in.

00:23:50.200 --> 00:23:53.680
Of course, right now, we are
doing some approximation.

00:23:53.680 --> 00:23:55.790
Because one of the
things, for example,

00:23:55.790 --> 00:23:57.340
we have to do to
score a message,

00:23:57.340 --> 00:24:00.560
was to, firstly, send
it to Translate API--

00:24:00.560 --> 00:24:03.050
so that's another Google
tool that we are using--

00:24:03.050 --> 00:24:06.850
get the translation back, and
then we score the translation.

00:24:06.850 --> 00:24:09.310
One thing that we did
to try to minimize

00:24:09.310 --> 00:24:11.260
the errors and
cultural difference

00:24:11.260 --> 00:24:13.840
was, when we had the
data set labeled,

00:24:13.840 --> 00:24:18.320
we had it labeled by people
that were from that culture.

00:24:18.320 --> 00:24:20.260
So we were splitting the data.

00:24:20.260 --> 00:24:23.950
So we were asking,
basically, Russian people

00:24:23.950 --> 00:24:27.160
to review Russian
messages, Italian people

00:24:27.160 --> 00:24:30.370
to review Italian messages,
and so on, to actually get

00:24:30.370 --> 00:24:32.890
an understanding
of the differences.

00:24:32.890 --> 00:24:35.600
Right now, what we are
really trying to do,

00:24:35.600 --> 00:24:37.480
which, I think,
kind of minimized

00:24:37.480 --> 00:24:39.880
the problem in our use
case, we are really

00:24:39.880 --> 00:24:43.510
trying to go after behavior
that are really, really toxic.

00:24:43.510 --> 00:24:45.590
So when we released
this first model

00:24:45.590 --> 00:24:47.590
into our production
system and then it

00:24:47.590 --> 00:24:50.255
started automatically
warning and banning people--

00:24:50.255 --> 00:24:51.880
to give you an idea,
we are, basically,

00:24:51.880 --> 00:24:55.570
targeting the top 5%
of most toxic behavior.

00:24:55.570 --> 00:24:59.050
So within that range, yes,
cultural differences are still

00:24:59.050 --> 00:25:00.550
there, but not as much.

00:25:00.550 --> 00:25:02.710
Because, on the
opposite side, I do

00:25:02.710 --> 00:25:05.530
think that a slight
bit of toxicity

00:25:05.530 --> 00:25:07.750
is part of competitiveness.

00:25:07.750 --> 00:25:11.320
It's like thinking that
football players would lightly

00:25:11.320 --> 00:25:13.030
insult themselves on the field.

00:25:13.030 --> 00:25:15.670
It's just feels like
it can get to a point

00:25:15.670 --> 00:25:17.940
where you're just, basically,
being the Big Brother,

00:25:17.940 --> 00:25:21.370
watching on everything,
and censoring everything.

00:25:21.370 --> 00:25:23.560
You still kind of need
to keep a balance.

00:25:23.560 --> 00:25:25.720
And you need,
also, to understand

00:25:25.720 --> 00:25:28.750
you can't go after
everything, because then it

00:25:28.750 --> 00:25:33.250
would kind of put yourself at
the opposite end, I would say.

00:25:33.250 --> 00:25:35.590
BRIAN DORSEY: You used
the word banter earlier.

00:25:35.590 --> 00:25:35.980
MARIA LAURA SCURI: Yeah.

00:25:35.980 --> 00:25:36.370
Exactly.

00:25:36.370 --> 00:25:37.940
BRIAN DORSEY: In a competition,
you've got this going on.

00:25:37.940 --> 00:25:38.140
MARIA LAURA SCURI: Yeah.

00:25:38.140 --> 00:25:40.390
So I think there is
some banter going on.

00:25:40.390 --> 00:25:43.720
And I think you don't
want I get into a position

00:25:43.720 --> 00:25:48.040
that your model is, actually,
like banning for banter.

00:25:48.040 --> 00:25:51.760
So you still want to
find that middle area.

00:25:51.760 --> 00:25:55.420
Then there were things that
were a bit tricky, for example,

00:25:55.420 --> 00:25:56.920
with using Perspective.

00:25:56.920 --> 00:26:01.450
Or, in general, if the gaming
language has a lot of slang.

00:26:01.450 --> 00:26:04.450
So that was one of
the tricky bits--

00:26:04.450 --> 00:26:07.260
to factor that slang
into our model.

00:26:07.260 --> 00:26:11.160
So there were some efforts
on that side that we put in.

00:26:11.160 --> 00:26:14.910
And then on the other side
are things that are not

00:26:14.910 --> 00:26:16.500
acceptable in other contexts.

00:26:16.500 --> 00:26:19.230
There are actually standard
terms in a game like

00:26:19.230 --> 00:26:21.450
"Counter-Strike:
Global Offensive."

00:26:21.450 --> 00:26:25.380
For example, if I say, nice
head shot in "Counter-Strike,"

00:26:25.380 --> 00:26:28.060
it's actually meant
to be a compliment.

00:26:28.060 --> 00:26:32.820
But outside of it, you
might be inciting violence.

00:26:32.820 --> 00:26:34.470
BRIAN DORSEY:
Context differences.

00:26:34.470 --> 00:26:39.310
So toxicity is a huge
challenge everywhere online.

00:26:39.310 --> 00:26:41.820
And I think many of the
folks who are listening

00:26:41.820 --> 00:26:43.770
are building and
running systems where

00:26:43.770 --> 00:26:45.120
users interact with each other.

00:26:45.120 --> 00:26:46.770
Do you have
recommendations for them

00:26:46.770 --> 00:26:48.478
on how to apply some
of the things you've

00:26:48.478 --> 00:26:49.740
learned in this space?

00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:50.740
MARIA LAURA SCURI: Yeah.

00:26:50.740 --> 00:26:52.440
I think it's the two things--

00:26:52.440 --> 00:26:56.160
on one side, before
using this approach,

00:26:56.160 --> 00:26:57.870
we were trying to
go down the approach

00:26:57.870 --> 00:27:01.080
that I think is used by many
that's more like building

00:27:01.080 --> 00:27:02.520
a karma my system.

00:27:02.520 --> 00:27:06.600
So it tries to get an index
that tries to represent

00:27:06.600 --> 00:27:09.630
how toxic of a player you are.

00:27:09.630 --> 00:27:12.330
I think, in general, a
karma system works good

00:27:12.330 --> 00:27:14.940
when you are trying to build
that as an honor score,

00:27:14.940 --> 00:27:17.100
like more of how good
of a player you are,

00:27:17.100 --> 00:27:19.950
rather than how bad
of a player you are.

00:27:19.950 --> 00:27:23.700
Or, in general, not just
in gaming, but everywhere.

00:27:23.700 --> 00:27:26.910
I think, on one side, it's
trying to enforce the positive.

00:27:26.910 --> 00:27:30.540
Then, on the other side, I
also think, know your context.

00:27:30.540 --> 00:27:32.190
So know what you're
dealing with.

00:27:32.190 --> 00:27:35.560
Because I think toxicity can
be so many different things.

00:27:35.560 --> 00:27:38.600
I mean, even on, let's
say, a social network,

00:27:38.600 --> 00:27:42.210
I can be toxic by writing
a very, very bad comment,

00:27:42.210 --> 00:27:45.120
or I can be toxic by
posting a bad image,

00:27:45.120 --> 00:27:49.270
or I could post a video
that is out of spite.

00:27:49.270 --> 00:27:51.810
And those are all like
different data signals.

00:27:51.810 --> 00:27:54.120
When I say, know your
context, it's also,

00:27:54.120 --> 00:27:57.400
know how toxicity
can express itself.

00:27:57.400 --> 00:28:00.310
And consider all these
different sources.

00:28:00.310 --> 00:28:03.090
And the fact is that you kind
of need to deal with all of them

00:28:03.090 --> 00:28:05.370
because, otherwise,
people are smart.

00:28:05.370 --> 00:28:09.690
So if they find out you
cannot write a swear word

00:28:09.690 --> 00:28:12.120
in your comment, they're
probably going to put a swear

00:28:12.120 --> 00:28:14.194
word in your image, if
they notice that detecting

00:28:14.194 --> 00:28:14.340
the image.

00:28:14.340 --> 00:28:14.550
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:28:14.550 --> 00:28:15.967
They'll figure
something else out.

00:28:17.212 --> 00:28:18.670
MARIA LAURA SCURI:
So they're going

00:28:18.670 --> 00:28:19.795
to think of something else.

00:28:19.795 --> 00:28:20.970
So you need to know them.

00:28:20.970 --> 00:28:23.910
And you need to try to use
as comprehensive an approach

00:28:23.910 --> 00:28:25.090
as you can.

00:28:25.090 --> 00:28:26.730
And then I think the
other side is also

00:28:26.730 --> 00:28:28.710
to celebrate the small wins.

00:28:28.710 --> 00:28:30.470
It's a huge issue.

00:28:30.470 --> 00:28:33.840
And I think he needs to be
broken down into smaller ones.

00:28:33.840 --> 00:28:37.590
And then you need to tackle each
smaller issue, one at a time.

00:28:37.590 --> 00:28:39.090
Because, otherwise,
if you're trying

00:28:39.090 --> 00:28:40.770
to just tackle
everything and then

00:28:40.770 --> 00:28:43.630
go out with this super big
system that tries to solve it

00:28:43.630 --> 00:28:47.070
all, you might not get the
result. Instead, I'm thinking,

00:28:47.070 --> 00:28:48.190
step by step.

00:28:48.190 --> 00:28:50.700
So just release the
first small thing.

00:28:50.700 --> 00:28:52.470
See, also, which affects it has.

00:28:52.470 --> 00:28:55.140
Because one thing
is, we are people.

00:28:55.140 --> 00:28:56.510
So we learn from things.

00:28:56.510 --> 00:29:01.080
So that's also one of the
things that you want to rely on.

00:29:01.080 --> 00:29:03.260
When you start
pushing for change,

00:29:03.260 --> 00:29:05.550
then, hopefully, people are
going to follow as well.

00:29:05.550 --> 00:29:08.180
So they are actually going to
help you in what you are doing.

00:29:08.180 --> 00:29:08.972
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:29:08.972 --> 00:29:11.850
So you, basically, said
think about behavior

00:29:11.850 --> 00:29:14.265
instead of reputation overall.

00:29:14.265 --> 00:29:15.890
So what people are
doing in the moment.

00:29:15.890 --> 00:29:17.410
And that helps with these
short term things as well.

00:29:17.410 --> 00:29:18.410
MARIA LAURA SCURI: Yeah.

00:29:18.410 --> 00:29:21.220
Because I also think that
it helps you in many ways.

00:29:21.220 --> 00:29:24.570
It helps into accepting
criticism better.

00:29:24.570 --> 00:29:26.670
So I think like that's
always something

00:29:26.670 --> 00:29:28.050
I have been thinking of.

00:29:28.050 --> 00:29:30.810
And that's why that
was one of the things

00:29:30.810 --> 00:29:33.430
that I really pushed
and FACEIT embraced.

00:29:33.430 --> 00:29:36.390
And I think we are, basically,
trying to build this.

00:29:36.390 --> 00:29:38.380
And then, hopefully,
maybe in the future,

00:29:38.380 --> 00:29:41.550
this can become a tool
that other game publishers.

00:29:41.550 --> 00:29:45.240
But it's also really
swapping from criticize you

00:29:45.240 --> 00:29:48.660
as a person, which is usually
not taken that well-- and also,

00:29:48.660 --> 00:29:50.700
who am I to criticize
you as a person?

00:29:50.700 --> 00:29:52.530
Maybe you are just
having a bad day.

00:29:52.530 --> 00:29:55.180
So I just want to point out
that you did something wrong.

00:29:55.180 --> 00:29:57.600
And I think that's
taken a lot better, also

00:29:57.600 --> 00:30:00.570
in a personal point
of view, in general.

00:30:00.570 --> 00:30:02.220
School or such is
very different,

00:30:02.220 --> 00:30:04.350
when you tell a kid
you did something wrong

00:30:04.350 --> 00:30:07.518
or, instead, you tell them,
well, you're being a bad kid.

00:30:07.518 --> 00:30:08.310
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:30:08.310 --> 00:30:11.160
It's really interesting to hear
about aligning the software

00:30:11.160 --> 00:30:13.410
systems to what's
actually just, basically,

00:30:13.410 --> 00:30:15.378
good, interpersonal,
human behavior.

00:30:15.378 --> 00:30:16.920
It's kind of a
classic recommendation

00:30:16.920 --> 00:30:18.900
to talk about the behavior
rather than the person.

00:30:18.900 --> 00:30:19.280
MARIA LAURA SCURI: Yeah.

00:30:19.280 --> 00:30:19.800
Exactly.

00:30:19.800 --> 00:30:22.320
I think what we're trying
to do is, actually--

00:30:22.320 --> 00:30:25.260
I think artificial intelligence
and machine learning

00:30:25.260 --> 00:30:27.390
are as smart as people are.

00:30:27.390 --> 00:30:29.390
Or they can be as
smart as people are.

00:30:29.390 --> 00:30:31.890
And it's not that artificial
intelligence is just

00:30:31.890 --> 00:30:32.850
this magical thing.

00:30:32.850 --> 00:30:34.860
It depends on how you build it.

00:30:34.860 --> 00:30:37.470
So what we are really trying
to build with Minerva as well

00:30:37.470 --> 00:30:41.310
is we can make her think
the way we think, in a way.

00:30:41.310 --> 00:30:43.890
So it's more like if
there are practices

00:30:43.890 --> 00:30:46.590
that are good in
human relations,

00:30:46.590 --> 00:30:48.600
why should we forget
about them when

00:30:48.600 --> 00:30:51.840
we are in a technological field
and we are trying to build.

00:30:51.840 --> 00:30:53.250
BRIAN DORSEY: Love it.

00:30:53.250 --> 00:30:55.150
Love it, love it, love it.

00:30:55.150 --> 00:30:56.910
So if people are
motivated and want

00:30:56.910 --> 00:30:58.830
to learn more about
this space, do you

00:30:58.830 --> 00:31:01.470
have recommendations
for where to go next?

00:31:01.470 --> 00:31:05.900
JON FOUST: Besides becoming
a super competitive gamer.

00:31:05.900 --> 00:31:06.930
MARIA LAURA SCURI: Yes.

00:31:06.930 --> 00:31:07.685
Play a lot.

00:31:07.685 --> 00:31:10.010
No.

00:31:10.010 --> 00:31:12.570
There's the case study
that was published today

00:31:12.570 --> 00:31:14.573
on the Jigsaw blog post.

00:31:14.573 --> 00:31:16.740
BRIAN DORSEY: We'll put a
link to that in the notes.

00:31:16.740 --> 00:31:17.740
MARIA LAURA SCURI: Yeah.

00:31:17.740 --> 00:31:21.900
Perspective API-- there's lots
of good documentation there.

00:31:21.900 --> 00:31:24.390
If you read about
toxicity in gaming,

00:31:24.390 --> 00:31:27.560
there's lots of articles
coming up about it.

00:31:27.560 --> 00:31:31.810
Actually, lots of studies from
researchers in academia about,

00:31:31.810 --> 00:31:35.430
in general, how to apply machine
learning and AI on toxicity.

00:31:35.430 --> 00:31:37.230
But as a general
model, and then you

00:31:37.230 --> 00:31:39.270
can always take that
learning and see

00:31:39.270 --> 00:31:41.430
how they fit in your own space.

00:31:41.430 --> 00:31:42.930
BRIAN DORSEY: Can
we ask you for one

00:31:42.930 --> 00:31:44.130
to three
recommendations we could

00:31:44.130 --> 00:31:46.430
put in the show notes for
specific papers to look at?

00:31:46.430 --> 00:31:46.710
MARIA LAURA SCURI: Yeah.

00:31:46.710 --> 00:31:47.210
Sure.

00:31:47.210 --> 00:31:48.510
I can send those over.

00:31:48.510 --> 00:31:49.290
JON FOUST: Is there
anything that we've

00:31:49.290 --> 00:31:51.390
missed that you would like to
mention before we wrap things

00:31:51.390 --> 00:31:51.930
up?

00:31:51.930 --> 00:31:53.347
Or would you like
to let listeners

00:31:53.347 --> 00:31:54.540
know where we can find you?

00:31:54.540 --> 00:31:56.613
Or we can find any material
that you're posting,

00:31:56.613 --> 00:31:58.530
so that we can stay up
to date with everything

00:31:58.530 --> 00:31:59.760
that FACEIT is doing?

00:31:59.760 --> 00:32:00.760
MARIA LAURA SCURI: Yeah.

